  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "How do you add two vectors?",
      "a": "Draw the first arrow, then start the second arrow at the nose of the first. The arrow from the very start to the final nose is the sum. With numbers, add the across parts and the up parts.",
      "asks": [
        "How do I add vectors?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is a resultant vector?",
      "a": "The resultant is the single vector that has the same effect as two or more vectors together. Here it is the boat's real path, 5 metres per second.",
      "asks": [
        "What does resultant mean?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why does a boat drift downstream when crossing a river?",
      "a": "The water carries the boat sideways while it rows across. The two motions add, so the boat moves at a slant.",
      "asks": [
        "Why does a boat not go straight across?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How do you add vectors with numbers?",
      "a": "Add matching parts. 0 across and 4 up, plus 3 across and 0 up, gives 3 across and 4 up.",
      "asks": [
        "How do you add column vectors?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How can the boat go straight across?",
      "a": "Point the boat partly upstream, so that part of its rowing cancels the river. Then the resultant points straight across.",
      "asks": [
        "How do you cross a river in a straight line?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "Vectors add nose to tail; the resultant goes from the start of the first to the end of the last.",
    "Perpendicular velocities of 4 m/s and 3 m/s give a resultant of 5 m/s."
  ],
